Not at all.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.300
We are simply reporting the realities contained within our source material to help you

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.700
understand the policy mechanisms, especially for this core eight-minute explanation

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.820
we're going to do on the Puerto Rican health care paradox.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.700
Let's start right there, because before we can analyze the band aid that the

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.940
CAA 2023 put on Puerto Rico, I mean, we really have to understand the wound.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:00.980
Yeah, the underlying structural issue.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:01.980
Right.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.660
The source material outlines this massive disparity between how Medicaid operates in

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.980
the 50 states versus how it operates in Puerto Rico.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:13.220
And that disparity, it really just comes down to how the federal government matches

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:14.980
local health care spending.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:15.980
How does that normally work?

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:21.380
Well, in the 50 states in Washington, D.C., federal Medicaid funding is open-ended.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.380
Okay.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.460
So the federal government matches state spending based on a specific formula that's

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.060
tied to the state's per capita income.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.380
And that's what they call the FMAP.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.380
Right.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.380
Exactly.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.540
The federal medical assistance percentage, the FMAP.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.940
Meaning if a state has a lower per capita income, the feds cover a higher

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.060
percentage of their Medicaid costs.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.060
Right.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:47.100
The system is designed to be responsive to the state's economic reality.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.100
Which makes sense.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.100
Right.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:55.380
It's this matching rate ranges from 50% for wealthier states up to a maximum of 83% for

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.380
the poorest states.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:57.380
So it just...

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.380
It does.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.020
If a state's Medicaid population grows, or if health care costs suddenly spike because

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.460
of a public health crisis...

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:04.460
Like COVID.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.460
Yeah.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.340
Exactly like COVID, the federal government automatically scales up its funding to cover

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.060
its percentage of those eligible costs.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.820
There's no statutory ceiling.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.260
But Puerto Rico is playing by an entirely different set of rules, aren't they?

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.260
Completely different.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.860
It's like they literally have the worst of both worlds when it comes to this funding

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:21.860
formula.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:22.860
They do.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.620
So Puerto Rico's Medicaid program is known as PlanVital.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.420
And it operates under two simultaneous, really strict statutory restrictions.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:31.420
Okay.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.420
What's the first one?

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.780
First, they are not subject to that open-ended match we just talked about.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.500
They have a strict, absolute annual cap on federal funds.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:40.500
A hard cap.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.500
A hard cap.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.660
And second, their statutory FMAP isn't responsive to their per capita income at all.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.580
It is fixed by law at a flat 55%.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:50.580
Wait.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:55.980
So the moment Puerto Rico hits that hard annual cap, the federal funding just shuts off for

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:56.980
the rest of the year.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:57.980
And it shuts off.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:02.060
And then the territory is just on the hook to pay 100% of the remaining Medicaid costs

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.740
out of their own local pocket.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:04.740
Exactly.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.660
The burden shifts entirely to the local government.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:12.900
And this structural design, it becomes incredibly glaring when you look at the demographic

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.260
data in the sources.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.340
Let's talk about the data because this blew my mind.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:17.340
Yeah.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:20.380
Puerto Rico has a poverty rate hovering around 40% to 43%.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:21.780
40% to 43%.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:22.780
Right.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.700
And to put that in perspective for you, the poorest of the 50 states is Mississippi,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.300
which has a poverty rate of about 19%.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.300
Wow.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.860
So it's more than double the poverty rate of the poorest U.S. state.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:34.860
Exactly.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:40.420
And as a result, Puerto Rico covers roughly 47% of its total population under Medicaid

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:44.060
and the Children's Health Insurance Program, or CHIP.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.220
Almost half the island.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.220
Almost half.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:49.540
And the national average in the states is only about 21%.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:54.220
So they have double the poverty rate and nearly half their entire population relying on the

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.700
safety net, but their funding is artificially capped.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:58.700
Yes.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.820
I mean, you know, it's kind of like a municipal government giving the family with the most

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:07.700
mouths to feed the absolute smallest, most strictly capped grocery budget in the whole

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:08.700
neighborhood.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:09.700
That's a good way to look at it.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:13.740
But then asking them to serve the exact same dinner as the wealthy families, it's

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:14.740
impossible.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:15.740
It is impossible.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.060
That was inevitably diverged.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:21.540
I mean, despite having double the need, the federal Medicaid grants per capita reflect

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.700
that artificial funding cap, not the actual poverty rate.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:26.220
What are the per capita numbers?

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:32.820
In fiscal year 2024, the federal government spent $1171 per capita on Medicaid in Puerto

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:33.820
Rico.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:34.820
Okay.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:35.820
And nationally?

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.100
Nationally, the average was $1,837 per capita.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.020
That's a massive gap.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:44.340
How does a healthcare system absorb a deficit like that?

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.940
Because, you know, they can't just run on a deficit indefinitely.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.060
They have to be cutting things.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.060
They are cutting things.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:54.180
And our source material details exactly what is missing from PlanVital.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.180
Because of this chronic underfunding, Puerto Rico operates without several mandatory

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.660
benefits that are completely standard across the 50 states.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:02.660
Like what?

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:03.660
What are they missing?

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:07.100
Well, for one, Puerto Rico's Medicaid program does not offer a nursing facility

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.100
care.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.100
None at all.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.100
None.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.700
They also have severely limited non-emergency medical transport.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.580
So just getting to a doctor is harder.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:15.580
Much harder.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.980
And they lack a full Medicare savings program.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:18.980
What is that?

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:19.980
Exactly.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.900
That's the system that helps seniors pay for their Medicare premiums and their out-of-pocket

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:23.900
costs.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.180
The funding simply isn't there to provide parity and care.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.700
Which brings us to the CAA 2023.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.300
Congress passes this massive appropriations bill, and part of its function was to address

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.700
these recurrent funding crises in Puerto Rico.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:37.700
Right.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:41.700
Did the law actually restructure the system, like did they fix the paradox?

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.580
No, they didn't alter the underlying statutory framework at all.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:46.580
Wait.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:47.580
Really?

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:53.980
What the CAA 2023 did was authorize a massive temporary infusion of cash to essentially just

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.060
buy five years of operational stability.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.060
Just five years.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.340
Okay, let's look at the mechanics of that cash infusion.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.060
Because they really opened the checkbook for a minute.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.060
They did.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.700
The law set these unusually high-based funding allocations for Puerto Rico for

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:10.820
the federal fiscal years 2023, all the way through 2027.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:18.420
It started at $3.275 billion in 2023, and it scales up to $3.825 billion by 2027.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:19.420
Right.

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:24.340
And crucially, it also bumped that flat 55% federal matching rate up to 76%.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:25.620
Which is huge for the local government.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:26.700
Oh, it's massive.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:30.460
The local financial oversight and management board was able to reduce the local funding

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:34.620
requirement by hundreds of millions of dollars almost immediately.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.900
But the legislation actually went further.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.420
It unlocked additional conditional funds tied to very specific policy outcomes.

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:45.860
Basically, Congress used the money to force systemic changes.

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.700
Let's break down those conditions.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.900
Because there's a $300 million bonus in there, right?

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:50.900
Yeah.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:56.900
Every single year from 2023 to 2027, Puerto Rico can unlock an extra $300 million.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.500
Provided they implement what's called a directed payment arrangement.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.260
And what does that actually mean for the doctors on the ground?

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.580
It mandates that the managed care organizations, you know, the private companies,

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:08.900
the government contracts to administer Medicaid.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:14.020
They have to pay doctors and medical providers at least 75% of the Medicare Part B rate.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.020
Okay.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:19.180
So the Medicare Part B rate is essentially the standard federal benchmark for outpatient

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.180
care.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.180
Exactly.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:25.580
So Congress is basically forcing the local private administrators to actually pay doctors

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:26.940
a fair wage.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.500
I'm assuming that's to stop the massive brain drain of medical professionals leaving

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.060
the island for the mainland.

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:33.540
That is the exact mechanism.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:37.460
By establishing a payment floor, they stabilize the provider network so doctors actually

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:38.940
stay in Puerto Rico.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:40.020
It's total sense.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:45.500
And there's also another $75 million available annually if Puerto Rico meets specific program

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:46.700
integrity requirements.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:47.700
Like oversight stuff.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:48.700
Yeah.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:49.700
Yeah.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.820
They had to designate a Medicaid program integrity lead and a contracting and procurement

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.820
oversight lead just to ensure all these new funds are managed transparently.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:58.820
And did they?

00:08:58.820 --> 00:08:59.820
They did.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:04.180
They activated all these conditions, bringing their potential five-year federal relief to

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.260
over $19 billion.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.060
$19 billion.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:10.620
That is a massive temporary rescue.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.180
But and here's the alarm bell we talked about at the beginning.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:16.460
This brings us to October 1st, 2027.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:17.820
The end of the five-year window.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:18.820
The fiscal cliff.

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:19.820
Right.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:25.180
The CAA 2023 mandates that for fiscal year 2028, Puerto Rico's Medicaid funding

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:31.500
cap is recalculated as if those elevated supplements from 2020 to 2027 never even happen.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:35.420
I got to pause you here because this is where the policy mechanics get really, really strange.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:36.420
They do.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:40.540
Why would a law artificially erase eight years of funding history?

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:44.340
It's like throwing someone a life raft but programming it to completely deflate right

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:45.540
before they reach the shore.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.540
Right.

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:50.940
Or like giving someone a subsidized mortgage but burying a clause that says, in five

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:55.900
years, your payment recalculates based on an imaginary housing market from 10 years ago.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:56.900
Right.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:59.700
Why design a law to actively ignore reality?

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:06.220
It comes down to congressional budgetary mechanics, specifically how legislation is scored.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:07.220
Scored by who?

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.860
The Congressional Budget Office, the CBO.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:14.940
When Congress drafts a bill, the CBO evaluates how much it will cost over a 10-year budget

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:15.940
window.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:16.940
I see where this is going.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:17.940
Right.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.180
If it were to make a multi-billion-dollar funding increase for Puerto Rico permanent,

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:26.380
it would score as massively expensive over that 10-year window, which makes the overall

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.220
bill way harder to pass.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:30.020
Because it looks too expensive on paper.

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:31.020
Exactly.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:35.660
By writing the law so the funding expires in five years and reverts back to historical

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.620
lows, the legislation looks significantly cheaper.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:43.420
It's really just a budgetary optical illusion that kicks the fiscal can down the road.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:48.780
So they architected a catastrophic cliff just to make the math work for a floor vote.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:49.780
Yes.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:56.460
And the consequences of that illusion becoming reality are staggering because the law requires

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:00.740
the 2028 cap to be based on the 2019 funding levels.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:04.900
Right, which were around $367 million plus inflation.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:09.220
So the CBO estimates this will drop Puerto Rico's annual Medicaid cap from around

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:14.500
$4 billion in 2027 down to roughly $500 million in 2028.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:15.500
Yes.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:21.140
And simultaneously, that federal matching rate reverts back from 76% to the flat 55%.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:24.420
I mean, a drop from $4 billion to $500 billion, that isn't a cliff.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:25.900
That's a systemic collapse.

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:26.900
It is.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:31.380
If Congress fails to pass another temporary patch or a permanent fix, what's the fallout

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.020
look like on the ground for you and me or people living there?

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:39.100
Well, the official estimates project Puerto Rico would lose roughly $3.5 billion in

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:40.540
federal funding overnight.

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:41.540
Overnight.

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:42.540
Yeah.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:46.420
The broader economic contraction for the territory is estimated at $5.4 billion.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:50.340
And they project the loss of 48,000 jobs across the health care sector and related

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:51.340
industries.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:52.340
That's devastating.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:53.780
It gets worse.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:59.180
Between 700,000 and 1 million people could lose their health care coverage entirely.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.980
The uninsured rate on the island would jump from 5.7% to nearly 30%.

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:06.220
30% uninsured.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:10.180
That level of systemic shock is just hard to comprehend.

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:14.100
But it also makes me wonder about the broader territorial landscape because the US has several

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:15.100
other territories.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:16.100
Yes, we do.

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:23.040
Did the CAA 2023 apply this same 2028 ticking clock to places like Guam or the US Virgin

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:24.040
Islands?

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:25.040
No.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.900
And this is where the legislation reveals a very specific asymmetry in how Congress approaches

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:30.580
territorial funding.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:36.100
The CAA 2023 treated Puerto Rico entirely differently than the other four territories.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:40.500
The Northern Mariana Islands, Guam, and the US Virgin Islands.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:43.740
How were those four smaller territories treated in the text of the wall?

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:48.980
The CAA actually made their maximum 83% federal matching rate permanent.

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:49.980
Permanent.

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:50.980
Permanent.

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:54.700
Furthermore, it allowed their elevated 2020 and 2021 funding caps to become their

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:57.060
new permanent statutory baseline.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:59.900
So those caps will now just grow steadily with inflation.

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:01.460
So they don't have a 2028 cliff.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:03.620
They face no cliff.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:07.100
Congress granted them permanent structural stability.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.620
Is that disparity simply a function of scale?

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:14.700
I mean, the population of Puerto Rico is over 3 million, whereas a territory like American

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:17.780
Samoa has what, roughly 45,000 people?

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:21.260
Scale is absolutely the driving factor in the budgetary math.

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:25.260
Puerto Rico accounts for about 91% of all territorial Medicaid spending.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:26.260
91%.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:27.260
Yeah.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:35.140
4.89 billion dollars for Puerto Rico in 2024 versus tens of millions for the smaller territories.

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:40.900
So fixing Puerto Rico permanently cost billions over a 10-year CBO scoring window.

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:43.060
Fixing the others costs a fraction of that.

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:45.180
So it's cheaper to fix the smaller ones permanently.

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:46.180
Right.

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:50.700
But from an impartial policy analysis standpoint, the result is that the largest and poorest

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:55.620
territory is left navigating this precarious temporary safety net while the smaller

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:59.660
territories were completely removed from the cycle of recurrent funding crises.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:02.780
The mechanics of how territories are treated is fascinating.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:06.380
But you know, the CAA 2023 didn't just deal with Puerto Rico and Guam.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:07.380
No, it didn't.

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:11.200
It fundamentally reshaped Medicaid for the rest of the country, too, for listeners

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.620
living right in the 50 states.

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:17.660
The source material outlines some major shifts in how vulnerable populations are

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:19.220
treated on the mainland.

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:20.220
It does.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:25.660
The scope of the CAA touches almost every corner of the national Medicaid landscape.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:30.120
And one of the most significant structural changes actually addresses the bureaucratic

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:34.140
friction that causes vulnerable people to lose coverage.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:37.460
Specifically, we're talking about coverage for children and postpartum mothers, right?

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:38.460
Exactly.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:42.820
The text mentions a mandate for 12 months of continuous eligibility for kids.

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:47.820
How did the system actually work before this law took effect in January 2024?

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:54.900
So previously, a child's enrollment in Medicaid, or CHIP, was highly, highly sensitive to monthly

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:55.900
income fluctuations.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:58.580
Like if the parents made a little too much one month.

00:14:58.580 --> 00:14:59.580
Right.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:03.460
If a parent picked up an extra shift at work or received a small seasonal bonus, their

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:07.660
monthly income might temporarily bump just above the eligibility threshold.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:09.100
And the state would just drop them.

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:11.420
The state would disenroll the child mid-year.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:15.700
This creates a phenomenon called churn where people constantly cycle on and off

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:17.040
the program.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:22.000
It disrupts their medical care, and it creates massive administrative backlogs for the state.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:26.080
So this new continuous eligibility basically eliminates that churn.

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:27.080
Yes.

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:32.640
The CAA now mandates that once a child under 19 is deemed eligible, they are locked into

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:38.680
coverage for a full 12 months completely regardless of minor income fluctuations in their household.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:40.200
That's a huge relief for parents.

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:41.200
It is.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:45.120
And the law also made permanent a state option to provide 12 months of continuous

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:46.880
postpartum coverage for mothers.

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:48.280
Which wasn't the standard before.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:49.280
No.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:53.680
Previously, pregnancy-related Medicaid often expired just 60 days after childbirth.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:54.680
Only two months.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:55.680
Yeah.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:16:00.640
So it is a major structural shift, prioritizing continuity of care over, you know, continuous

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.480
income verification.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:07.200
And that same logic of creating stability really seems to apply to the changes the law made

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.200
for the justice system too.

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:13.840
Because the shifts in how Medicaid handles incarcerated juveniles really stood out

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:15.880
in the reports as a major pivot.

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:17.400
It's a massive pivot.

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:24.080
It addresses a systemic friction point that stems from the Medicaid inmate exclusion policy,

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:26.240
which is a rule dating back to 1965.

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:27.240
Okay.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:28.240
What did that rule do?

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:32.880
Historically, federal Medicaid funds simply cannot pay for healthcare for inmates.

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:33.880
Right.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:38.000
The original intent there was to prevent states from, like, shifting the cost of prison

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:39.800
healthcare onto the federal government.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:40.800
Exactly.

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:44.680
But in practice, many states would just outright terminate a juvenile's Medicaid

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:47.640
enrollment the very second they entered a public institution.

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:51.760
We just, when that juvenile serves their time and is finally released, they step back into

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:54.240
the community with zero healthcare coverage.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:55.240
Zero.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:58.720
And they have to start the entire bureaucratic application process from scratch.

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:04.080
And that gap in coverage upon release that often leads directly to recidivism, right?

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:08.120
Especially if the juvenile has behavioral health or substance use issues that go untreated.

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:09.120
Absolutely.

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:14.800
CAA 2023 prohibits states from terminating Medicaid or CHIP for eligible juveniles pending

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.560
disposition of charges.

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:19.720
Instead, states must suspend the coverage.

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:22.960
So a suspension just means the switch is flipped back on the moment they walk out

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:23.960
the door.

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:24.960
Correct.

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.880
But the law actually goes further than that.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:31.760
It requires states to provide medical, dental, and behavioral health screenings as well

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:37.680
as targeted case management in the 30 days prior to the juvenile's scheduled release.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:39.920
So they're preparing them before they even leave.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:40.920
Exactly.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:45.600
It forces the state to build a runway ensuring health and social supports are active the second

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.920
the juvenile re-enters society.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:52.040
And that ties directly into the mental health funding outlined in the bill, doesn't it?

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:58.520
The CAA appropriated $8 million for a technical assistance center to help states build

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:00.440
out crisis response services.

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:01.440
Yeah.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:04.960
The whole focus of that funding is shifting behavioral health care away from the emergency

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:05.960
room.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:10.080
The emergency departments are generally terrible environments for someone experiencing a mental

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:11.080
health crisis.

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:12.080
Totally.

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:16.440
The technical assistance center is designed to help states build a true continuum of care.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:22.640
So supporting 988 crisis call centers, deploying mobile crisis teams directly to the individual,

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:26.480
and really expanding community-based de-escalation services.

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:32.800
So we are seeing a real expansion of the safety net in targeted areas, kids, mothers,

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:34.440
juveniles, mental health.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:40.120
But the CAA also brought the hammer down on the massive pandemic era of funding expansion.

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:41.960
This is the part called the COVID Unwind.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:42.960
Right.

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:43.960
The Unwind.

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:49.000
During the public health emergency, the federal government gave states a 6.2% increase in

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:50.720
their FMAP.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:52.840
But it came with a major string attached.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:54.440
What was the string?

00:18:54.440 --> 00:18:58.120
States were prohibited from kicking anyone off the Medicaid rolls even if their

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:01.720
income had increased and they were no longer technically eligible.

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:03.760
So nobody lost coverage during COVID.

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:08.520
The CAA formally decoupled that funding from the emergency declaration.

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:14.480
It mandated a phase down of that 6.2% increase, ending it entirely by December 31st, 2023.

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:15.480
Yes.

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:19.240
And as the extra federal cash phased out, states were required to begin the massive

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:23.760
logistical undertaking of redetermining the eligibility of every single person on their

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:24.760
Medicaid rolls.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:26.320
Which sounds like an administrative nightmare.

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:27.600
Oh, it was.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:33.240
But to prevent states from just blindly purging millions of people due to missing

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:37.920
paperwork, the CAA instituted intense transparency measures.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:39.040
Like what?

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:44.120
States had to submit monthly reports detailing exactly how many renewals they initiated, how

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:48.800
many people were terminated for procedural reasons versus actual ineligibility, and

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:51.240
they even had to report their call center wait times.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:52.240
Oh, wow.

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:53.240
Call center wait times.

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:54.240
Yeah.

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:58.640
And failure to report accurately resulted in federal funding penalties.

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:02.640
There's actually another transparency measure in the law regarding finding a doctor that

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:07.620
I want to touch on, because anyone who has ever tried to find an in-network specialist has

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:12.680
experienced the frustration of calling a dozen doctors from a list only to find out none

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:15.520
of them actually take the insurance or the numbers are disconnected.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:16.520
Yeah.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:20.160
You were describing a ghost network, and the CAA targets this directly.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:25.120
It mandates that Medicaid and CHAP managed care entities maintain accurate searchable

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:27.440
provider directories on a public website.

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:29.320
Which seems like the bare minimum.

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:30.320
It does.

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:33.560
But crucially, these must now be updated at least quarterly.

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:37.100
They have to show if a doctor is actively accepting new patients, they have to list their

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:41.480
cultural and linguistic capabilities, and they have to state whether their office has

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:43.680
accommodations for physical disabilities.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:48.320
So the COVID era continuous enrollment is over, but the baseline safety net for vulnerable

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:52.880
populations got structurally stronger, and the burden on the private organizations

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:55.560
managing the care just got a lot heavier.

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:56.560
Definitely.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:59.360
Well, we've covered a massive amount of legislative ground today.

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:04.120
From the shifting safety nets across the 50 states, to the multi-billion dollar ticking

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:05.840
clock in Puerto Rico.

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:08.920
So for you, the listener, how do you apply this information?

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:11.920
What are the actual takeaways from this deep dive?

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:15.800
Based purely on the mechanisms and data in our source material, we can break down the

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:19.720
actionable recommendations into three distinct categories.

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:23.280
For the government, for the private sector, and for the beneficiaries themselves.

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:24.280
Perfect.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:25.880
Let's look at the government strategy first.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:31.080
For the government in Puerto Rico, the strategy is aggressive preparation and lobbying.

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:35.980
They must continue executing their strategy, which is officially designated as Executive

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:42.320
Order 2026006 to present a unified, data-driven front in Washington.

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:43.480
And what's their goal there?

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:45.400
The fiscal goal is clear.

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:52.480
Secure a permanent 83% FMAP and a sustainable funding base of $4.4 billion before the

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:55.640
2028 cliff recalculation hits.

00:21:56.400 --> 00:22:02.000
Conversely, the federal government must address this structural cliff to prevent the unprecedented

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.800
collapse of a territorial health system.

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:08.760
A logical policy step, given the precedent we talked about, would be aligning Puerto

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:12.480
Rico with the permanent fixes already granted to the other four territories.

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:13.480
Makes sense.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:14.480
Now what about the private sector?

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:17.120
You know, the health care providers and the managed care organizations operating

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:18.400
under all these new rules.

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:19.400
Right.

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:22.640
For private providers operating in Puerto Rico, they must aggressively maximize the

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:27.840
current 75% Medicare Part B payment floor while the federal funds support it.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:29.000
While the money is there.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:30.000
Exactly.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:34.360
And nationally, managed care organizations need to audit their provider directories to

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:39.120
ensure they are strictly up to code with the new quarterly transparency requirements

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:41.600
because federal oversight is tightening.

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:42.600
For sure.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:47.360
Additionally, any private health care entity, hospital network, or insurer exposed to the

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:51.880
Puerto Rican market, must financially model and prepare their networks for extreme

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:57.240
economic volatility in late 2027 if Congress fails to enact a fix.

00:22:57.240 --> 00:23:02.400
Finally, what does this mean for the beneficiaries, the actual people relying on this

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.160
legislation for their health care?

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:08.200
If you are a beneficiary on the mainland, you need to be aware of your expanded rights

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:12.920
regarding the new 12 month continuous coverage for children and the extended postpartum

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:13.920
coverage.

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:14.920
Right.

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:16.440
Ensuring you don't accept an unlawful mid-year termination.

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:17.440
Exactly.

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:21.040
Now, if you reside in Puerto Rico, there is a very specific stringent requirement

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:22.560
you must prepare for.

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:27.880
The law mandates the implementation of a new asset verification system, or AVS, which goes

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:30.240
live on January 1st, 2026.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:31.920
This stood out to me.

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:36.160
The law essentially threatens to cut Puerto Rico's funding if they don't implement

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:38.640
this system to audit people's bank accounts.

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:40.720
That is the enforcement mechanism, yes.

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:45.440
The AVS will rigorously check financial accounts to verify Medicaid eligibility,

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:50.080
and it carries a potential 60 month look back period to ensure assets weren't

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:52.840
improperly transferred just to qualify for care.

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:54.360
60 months, so five years.

00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:55.360
Five years.

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:59.800
Beneficiaries must ensure their financial records are meticulously accurate and

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:04.200
transparent right now to avoid losing coverage when the system activates.

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:07.240
It is such a profound paradox, you know.

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:10.560
Tightening the bureaucratic rules and implementing these strict asset

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:14.480
verifications on one end while hurtling toward an existential funding

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:15.480
cliff on the other.

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:16.280
It really is.

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:19.880
Which leaves us with a final provocative question for you to mull over.

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:24.680
It isn't explicitly detailed in the congressional reports, but it is the logical

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:27.760
conclusion of the demographic data we've analyzed today.

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:33.040
If Congress allows the 2028 cliff to happen causing a $5.4 billion

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:36.320
economic contraction and leaving up to a million American citizens in

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:40.720
Puerto Rico suddenly without health care or jobs, what is the ripple effect on

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:44.360
the mainland US health care system when tens of thousands of those citizens

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:48.080
inevitably migrate to the 50 states seeking the care they can no longer

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:49.120
access at home?

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:52.040
The localized policy cliff rarely stays localized.

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:54.240
The system is entirely interconnected.

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:55.720
Thank you for taking this deep dive with us.

00:24:55.720 --> 00:24:57.480
Keep listening to the Health Care Paradox.

